Ritemark UI Layout: what sits where (sidebar, titlebar, explorer, panels, places row)
Sprint 38+: Original consolidated UI layout patch.
Sprint 53: Chrome activity bar refactor (28px action labels, 40px container).
Sprint 54: Agent Library activity bar entry, auxiliary bar chrome polish.
Sprint 55: Rebased onto VS Code 1.117.0 (CSS var migration, compact-mode constants).
v1.14.0 (Jarmo, 2026-10-13): Activity Bar order Home, Explorer, Tasks, ..., Agent Library last of Ritemark's.
Sprint 132 (Ritemark 2.0 step 1, 2026-10-17): this patch keeps placement; 018 and 019 own sizes and drawing.
  Moved out: the tab height (editorTabsControl.ts, now 019), the tree's row height (explorerViewer.ts and the
  height lines of explorerviewlet.css, now 018) and the places bar's injected 40px styles (paneCompositePart.ts,
  now 018's stylesheet).
  Removed: the terminal guards. A terminal is a tab (Jarmo, 2026-10-17), so terminalConfigurationService.ts and
  its test, terminalEditorSerializer.ts and terminalView.ts are upstream again, and terminal.contribution.ts keeps
  only the removal of the Toggle Terminal shortcut and View-menu entry. View > Advanced loses its Terminal entry.
  Changed: the places row's order is Home, Documents, Tasks, Browser, Agents, Transcribe, then the rest
  (viewsExtensionPoint.ts orders; compositeBar.ts applies it once to an older profile, ritemark.places.order.v2,
  and puts a new Ritemark place in its spot); Copilot Chat's launcher is always in More, after the places that do
  not fit (compositeBar.ts); the title bar's left-column toggle is off, because the column's own strip holds it
  (layoutActions.ts).
  In the places row only Ritemark's six take a spot: every other place lives in More, even while open, and More
  takes the chosen fill then; a place new to a saved row never goes in among the six (compositeBar.ts). Documents
  stays a place while its tree is not registered yet, and is called Documents (explorerViewlet.ts).
Sprint 135: rebased onto VS Code 1.142.0. The places row and the 40-high header, title and activity bar as before;
  compositeSize is upstream's (actionHeight + actionGap, 40 with Modern UI off, which product.json pins); the
  Appearance submenu stays out of View; layoutActions.ts also drops its KeyMod/KeyCode import, used at 1.142 only
  by the Cmd+B binding this patch removes.
Sprint 142 (2.1 polish, #557, Q4): View ▸ Advanced's Source Control entry reads File changes, its name everywhere (018
  names the view container).
Sprint 145 (#569): the AI column comes back with the layout in a project window (layout.ts). Ritemark AI is the column's
  default (paneCompositePart.ts), so VS Code never stores its id, and the layout read it before the extension's container
  was registered: the column started hidden and the extension opened it about half a second after Home, which then laid
  out twice. Now a project window restores Ritemark AI there, and VS Code's "the column was empty before" rule (which only
  recorded that race) no longer hides a new project's column. When nothing could be opened there once the extensions
  are read (Ritemark AI never registered), a project window's column closes again; and the empty-desk rule that gives the AI column the
  keyboard applies only to a window with no folder (the extension puts a new project's keyboard on Home). A window with
  no folder keeps upstream's rules.
Sprint 152 (the startup contract, R1–R4, R9): the shell draws Ritemark's frame from its first frame. ritemarkFrame.ts (new)
  reads product.json's ritemarkFrame block (branding/product.json) at the first frame: the places (ids, order, titles,
  icons, the head's rows), Home's place, the AI column, the permanent tabs, the desk's front, the welcome and the
  extension's id. Unknown keys are ignored; no block reads as no frame, logged once. It also holds the slots: a box
  drawn before its content exists, released once (logged "[ritemark] slot released: <name>") when the extension can't
  fill it, a contribution's slot when the extensions are read and a content slot when Ritemark isn't there or hasn't started after
  60 s; and the first frame's notes for the parts drawn after the layout (the place to restore, the welcome alone), a
  new profile's places as placeholders (R9) and a project's first desk (R3), both in VS Code's own stored forms. The
  place order (viewsExtensionPoint.ts) and the AI column's id (layout.ts, paneCompositePart.ts, auxiliaryBarPart.ts)
  read the manifest, where they were constants. layout.ts: a project window opens on its stored Ritemark place as VS
  Code's default one is restored (a built app restored no other at a new start), or on Home's place when it is opened
  for the first time (R3; VS Code stores nothing for Documents, its default place); the welcome's window, with no folder and nothing to open, starts with both columns closed (R4); the AI
  column's release is the slot's, and the shell's closing doesn't last: a note brings the column back with the layout
  at the next start, and only the person's toggle closes it for good (PR 1's review, finding 3; the comment no longer
  says the extension opens it again, which it stopped doing in Sprint 152's PR 1).
  Test: workbench/test/browser/ritemarkFrame.test.ts (the reader, the release log, the places' placeholders, the first
  desk; ./scripts/test.sh --grep Ritemark).
